  "question": {
    "text": "Let the range of the function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2+\\sin 3 x+\\cos 3 x}, x \\in \\mathbb{R}$ be $[a, b]$. If $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are respectively the A.M. and the G.M. of $a$ and $b$, then $\\frac{\\alpha}{\\beta}$ is equal to",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "hvkr1",
      "text": "$\\pi$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "R0dRr",
      "text": "$\\sqrt{\\pi}$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "9Rglp",
      "text": "2",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "qyHr1",
      "text": "$\\sqrt{2}$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "$\\begin{aligned} & f(x) \\frac{1}{2+\\sin 3 x+\\cos 3 x} \\\\ & {\\left[\\frac{1}{2+\\sqrt{2}}, \\frac{1}{2-\\sqrt{2}}\\right]} \\\\ & \\frac{\\alpha}{\\beta}=\\frac{a+b}{2 \\sqrt{a b}}=\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\sqrt{\\frac{a}{b}}+\\sqrt{\\frac{b}{a}}\\right) \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\sqrt{\\frac{2-\\sqrt{2}}{2+\\sqrt{2}}}+\\sqrt{\\frac{2+\\sqrt{2}}{2-\\sqrt{2}}}\\right) \\\\ & =\\frac{(2-\\sqrt{2})+(2+\\sqrt{2})}{2 \\times \\sqrt{2}}=\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}$",
    "image": null
  },
